---
scope: src/service/install/provisionBrain
description: >
  Plans brain config scaffolding at `.aide/config/brain.aide` (whose body
  carries four named sections — `prose`, `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`,
  `research` — delimited by paired HTML-comment markers), brain root
  directories, the playbook entry-point artifact, the study-playbook
  entry-point artifact, the research entry-point artifact, and the `brain`
  MCP server entry as six InitStep items — writes nothing to disk directly.
  Sources entry-point artifact bytes from the scaffolded brain.aide via
  parseBrainAide rather than from inline code constants. Is the install
  pipeline's one-place migration surface for legacy `obsidian`-keyed
  installs to the fixed `brain` key. The MCP entry is derived from the
  scaffolded brain.aide via parseBrainAide, never constructed inline.
intent: >
  provisionBrain's primary output is the scaffolded `.aide/config/brain.aide` —
  the canonical user-editable config file that becomes the single source of
  truth for how the brain layer is wired AND for the byte-level content of
  the three entry-point artifacts the framework requires. Given a project
  root, a resolved brain root, and an MCP config path, it plans six
  InitStep items in fixed order: the brain config file (under
  `.aide/config/`, the user-owned directory whose ownership boundary is
  inherited verbatim from the root spec), brain root directory creation,
  the playbook entry-point artifact, the study-playbook entry-point
  artifact, the research entry-point artifact, and the brain MCP server
  entry. The brain.aide body carries four named marker-bounded sections —
  `prose` (agent-facing usage instructions), `playbook` (the bytes
  scaffolded as the playbook entry-point artifact), `studyPlaybook` (the
  bytes scaffolded as the study-playbook entry-point artifact, holding the
  backend-specific playbook-navigation prose — Step 1/2/3 process,
  Navigation Rules with the depth-counting example, link-traversal
  semantics — that the `study-playbook` skill points at), and `research`
  (the bytes scaffolded as the research entry-point artifact) — and
  provisionBrain reads `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, and `research` via
  parseBrainAide to source the entry-point artifact bytes. The package
  does not own that content as inline TypeScript constants; brain.aide is
  the source of truth for both the launcher configuration and the
  entry-point artifact bodies. The MCP step's prescription is derived from
  the scaffolded brain.aide bytes via parseBrainAide and interpolateArgs —
  this module composes the InitStep for the MCP write but does not own its
  byte-level construction. provisionBrain is also the one-place migration
  surface for legacy installs that wrote the entry under the old
  `obsidian` key: it emits a `would-overwrite` step that moves the entry
  to the fixed `brain` key during the next `/aide:upgrade` or
  `/aide:init`. A new brain must be structurally complete on first run:
  directory scaffolding alone is not enough — all three entry-point
  artifacts must also be present so agents can reach every required
  surface without the user having to manually seed content first. The
  framework's brain contract is exactly three required entry-point
  artifacts (playbook + study-playbook + research) — nothing else.
  Existing files at any of the seed paths are never overwritten; the rule
  applies file-by-file at the brain root and directory-wide for everything
  under `.aide/config/`. All code identifiers and prose in this module
  refer to the brain in storage-agnostic terms — "playbook entry-point
  artifact", "study-playbook entry-point artifact", and "research
  entry-point artifact" are structural concepts, not connector-specific
  files; how they manifest (Markdown files in an editor-backed brain, pages
  in a hosted knowledge base, plain folders) is the user's brain.aide
  concern, not this package's. The default scaffolded brain.aide ships an
  Obsidian-flavored body because that is the most common storage choice,
  but the bundled template is the default, never a code-level special
  case. The new `studyPlaybook` body section's bytes are user-owned the
  moment they land, and the bundled obsidian-flavored default legitimately
  ships obsidian-flavored navigation prose inside those bytes per the
  parent intent's storage-agnostic-vocabulary carve-out for the
  user-owned section bytes; provisionBrain's code surface stays
  storage-agnostic regardless.
