      is a load-bearing user-owned surface inherited from the root spec.
      Per-file allowlists are forbidden; the directory boundary is the
      ownership signal, and any code path that touches a file under
      `.aide/config/` after its first write — even to \"fix\" a stale
      template, land newer defaults, or tidy formatting — is rejected."
    - "Writing a brain.aide whose schema borrows intent-spec frontmatter
      conventions — no `scope`, no `outcomes`, no `intent`, no `status`
      lifecycle field. brain.aide is a config file, not an intent spec;
      conflating the two erodes the distinction every downstream agent
      depends on."
    - >-
      "A brain root that has directories but no playbook entry-point
      artifact, no study-playbook entry-point artifact, or no research
      entry-point artifact. Agents invoking the study-playbook skill will
      find no on-disk navigation prose to follow; agents drilling into
      coding conventions from the playbook entry-point will find no
      conventions; agents resolving research lookups will find no research
      entry-point at all. All three required entry-point artifacts must
      ship together for the brain to satisfy the framework contract."
    - >-
      "A playbook or research entry-point artifact whose body section in
      brain.aide invents domain content, routing entries, or specific
      links for the user. The bundled default enforces structure
      (headings, table skeleton, placeholder prose) for these two
      sections and lets the user fill in domain-specific content — never
      the other way around. The studyPlaybook section is governed by a
      different rule: its bytes ARE package-authored navigation
      methodology shipped through the bundled obsidian-flavored template
      (the Step 1/2/3 process, Navigation Rules with the depth-counting
      example, link-traversal semantics — content the package owns by
      design because it travels with the bundled default). The user is
      free to override the studyPlaybook bytes for a different brain;
      the prohibition applies to playbook and research, where the user's
      domain content goes."
    - An InitStep that writes to disk directly. provisionBrain is a
      planner; any step that touches the filesystem violates the
      architectural contract between planning helpers and the apply layer.
---

## Context

provisionBrain plans the brain-layer scaffolding the AIDE pipeline
needs on first install. The framework's brain contract is exactly
three required entry-point artifacts — a playbook entry-point
artifact, a study-playbook entry-point artifact, and a research
entry-point artifact — plus the MCP wiring that connects the host's
agents to whatever knowledge store backs them. provisionBrain's job
is to plan six InitStep items that together stand up that contract:
the user-editable `brain.aide` config file, the brain root directory
tree, the playbook entry-point artifact, the study-playbook
entry-point artifact, the research entry-point artifact, and the
brain MCP server entry. It writes nothing to disk — every step is a
planning artifact the install service's apply layer turns into bytes.

The load-bearing decision is where the byte-level content for those
artifacts comes from. The legacy module owned the launcher shape, the
playbook entry-point body, and the brain-root CLAUDE.md body as inline
TypeScript constants in `index.ts`. The new shape moves all of that
content into the user's own `brain.aide` body and reads it back
through `parseBrainAide`. The default scaffolded `brain.aide` ships
four named marker-bounded body sections — a `prose` section
(agent-facing usage instructions, delimited by
`<!-- aide-prose-start -->` / `<!-- aide-prose-end -->`), a `playbook`
section (the bytes scaffolded as the playbook entry-point artifact,
delimited by `<!-- aide-playbook-start -->` /
`<!-- aide-playbook-end -->`), a `studyPlaybook` section (the bytes
scaffolded as the study-playbook entry-point artifact, delimited by
`<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` /
`<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->`), and a `research` section (the
bytes scaffolded as the research entry-point artifact, delimited by
`<!-- aide-research-start -->` / `<!-- aide-research-end -->`) — and
the parser exposes each section as its own typed field. provisionBrain
reads `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, and `research` to populate the
entry-point artifact steps; the package no longer owns the bytes
those steps deliver.

The `playbook` body section trims to pure coding conventions in the
new shape — the Task Routing table, Always Read First, Sections,
Contents — and nothing else. The multi-month-tested
playbook-navigation prose (the Step 1/2/3 process for using the
playbook entry-point, Navigation Rules with the depth-counting
example, link-traversal semantics) moves out of `playbook` and into
the new `studyPlaybook` section. The trim is load-bearing: it makes
the `playbook` section single-purpose (pure conventions, no
procedural navigation guidance interleaved with content rules) and
the `studyPlaybook` section single-purpose (procedural navigation
methodology), so each section's content is trimmable and overridable
without conflating concerns.

The new `studyPlaybook` section exists because the navigation prose
needs to travel with the user-owned brain template rather than the
package's release cycle. Shipping the navigation methodology as
brain.aide body bytes means it can evolve as the user's brain shape
evolves without requiring a code release: the `study-playbook` skill
on the agent side becomes minimal (a pointer that reads the on-disk
artifact at the brain's well-known path), and the navigation
methodology lives in the user's `brain.aide` and the artifact it
scaffolds. The bundled obsidian-flavored default ships the
multi-month-tested navigation prose written for an editor-backed
brain inside those bytes; per the parent intent's storage-agnostic
vocabulary carve-out for the user-owned `studyPlaybook` section
bytes, that prose legitimately uses the bundled template's connector
vocabulary because the bundled template ships that connector. The
carve-out applies to the section bytes (which become an on-disk
artifact the user can edit the moment they land); provisionBrain's
code surface — identifiers, comments, log strings, InitStep `name`
fields — stays storage-agnostic regardless.

The brain.aide path lives under `.aide/config/`, the host's user-owned
configuration directory established by the root spec. The ownership
rule is directory-wide: any file scaffolded into `.aide/config/` is
owned by the user the moment it lands, and neither aide_init nor
aide_upgrade overwrites, patches, or migrates anything inside that
directory on subsequent runs. brain.aide is the current inhabitant; the
convention is forward-compatible with future user-owned config files,
and provisionBrain inherits the structural rule rather than restating
a per-file invariant.

provisionBrain is also the one-place migration surface for the fixed
`brain` MCP key. Legacy installs that wrote the entry under the old
`obsidian` key are migrated here and nowhere else: when a legacy
`obsidian`-only entry is present, the MCP step reports
`would-overwrite` with key `brain`, so the next `/aide:upgrade` or
`/aide:init` moves the entry. The both-keys-present transitional state
is handled the same way. There is no runtime fallback to the legacy
key elsewhere in the codebase.

The framework's brain contract does NOT include a brain-root
CLAUDE.md. A previous shape returned a CLAUDE.md scaffolding step;
that step has been retired and the framework's brain contract is
exactly three required entry-point artifacts (playbook +
study-playbook + research) — nothing else. Users who want a
CLAUDE.md inside their brain are free to add one — it is their brain
— but the framework does not require it, and provisionBrain emits no
InitStep for it. Reintroducing a CLAUDE.md scaffolding step under any
name — "brain-root CLAUDE.md", "navigation entry-point", or any
other rename — restores the retired contract and is rejected. (The
token "vault" appears here only to forbid the retired naming, per
the root spec's anti-example carve-out.) The methodology guidance
that used to live in that file (link-crawling protocols, decision
protocols, where-to-find-things tables) now flows through the
`studyPlaybook` body section and the on-disk artifact it scaffolds;
the strategist owns the prose, the bundled template ships it, and
the install module reads it back through parseBrainAide rather than
baking it into install code.

## Strategy

**Six planning steps in fixed order: brain config → brain root
directories → playbook entry-point artifact → study-playbook
entry-point artifact → research entry-point artifact → MCP entry.**
Each is its own InitStep with its own idempotency check, and the
order is load-bearing: brain config is planned first because every
step downstream of it derives content from the bytes it scaffolds.
The three entry-point artifact steps come before the MCP step
because the parser pass that powers all four reads the same
brain.aide; running parseBrainAide once after the brain config step
is resolved keeps the orchestrator's flow linear. The study-playbook
step sits between playbook and research so the artifact-step order
mirrors the body-section order (prose-then-playbook-then-
studyPlaybook-then-research) — the same parser pass walks the
sections in declared order and emits one step per artifact-bearing
section without any reordering layer in between.

**brain.aide is the single source of truth for both launcher bytes
and entry-point artifact bytes.** The load-bearing structural change
is that the default scaffolded brain.aide ships four named
marker-bounded body sections — `prose` (agent-facing usage
instructions), `playbook` (the playbook entry-point artifact body),
`studyPlaybook` (the study-playbook entry-point artifact body), and
`research` (the research entry-point artifact body) — and every
artifact step downstream sources its `content` from the corresponding
section via parseBrainAide. The MCP step calls parseBrainAide (or its
in-memory string sibling for the cold-install path where brain.aide
has not yet hit disk) and then `interpolateArgs` to compute the
entry's command and args. The entry-point artifact steps call the
same parser and read `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, and `research` for
their bytes. No artifact step hardcodes a launcher shape, a playbook
structure, a study-playbook structure (the navigation methodology
that ships in the bundled obsidian default's `studyPlaybook`
section), or a research structure as an inline TypeScript constant
— every byte the apply layer eventually writes for these artifacts
traces back to a body section the user owns and can edit. A user
edit to any section flows into the next install or sync
automatically; the package no longer competes with the user for
ownership of any of these bytes.

**Body sections are marker-bounded with a closed marker set; parsed
fields are `prose`, `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, and `research`.**
The parser enforces four required marker pairs in fixed order:
`<!-- aide-prose-start -->` / `<!-- aide-prose-end -->`,
`<!-- aide-playbook-start -->` / `<!-- aide-playbook-end -->`,
`<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` /
`<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->`, and
`<!-- aide-research-start -->` / `<!-- aide-research-end -->` —
recognized lowercase and case-sensitive in the prose-then-playbook-
then-studyPlaybook-then-research order. The parsed result on the
`ok` branch carries each section as its own typed field whose name
matches the marker token base name verbatim (kebab-case
`study-playbook` markers populate the camelCase `studyPlaybook`
field). Marker layout violations (a missing pair, a typo'd token, an
unmatched closer, an opener without a closer, wrong section order,
nested markers) return `malformed-body` naming the violating marker
— there is no recovery, no auto-fill, no silent default. Bytes
outside any marker pair are silently ignored by the parser; users
may keep notes-to-self or scratch text in those regions. The closed
marker set means provisionBrain reaches for the section it owns by
name and never re-implements body grammar.

**The bundled-default-brain.aide template helper keeps its current
name and folder, but its responsibilities expand.** The helper at
`obsidianBrainAideTemplate/` now owns three things: the collapsed
two-field frontmatter, the platform-branched launcher invocation
(POSIX `npx ...` vs Windows `cmd /c npx ...`), AND the full byte
content of all four marker-bounded body sections — the playbook
section bytes (now trimmed to pure coding conventions: Task Routing,
Always Read First, Sections, Contents — nothing else), the
studyPlaybook section bytes (the multi-month-tested
backend-specific playbook-navigation prose written for an
editor-backed brain: the Step 1/2/3 process, the Navigation Rules
block with the depth-counting example, link-traversal semantics),
and the research section bytes. The trim is load-bearing in two
directions: the `playbook` section is now single-purpose (pure
conventions, no procedural navigation interleaved with content
rules), and the `studyPlaybook` section is single-purpose
(procedural navigation methodology with no content rules in it) —
separating them lets each section be trimmed or overridden without
conflating concerns. The helper name remains intentional because
the scaffolded template content is Obsidian-flavored — the prose
names the `mcp__brain__read_note` and `mcp__brain__search_notes`
tools, the playbook and research section bodies are written as
Markdown for an editor-backed brain, the studyPlaybook section
bytes legitimately ship the bundled template's connector vocabulary
under the parent intent's user-owned-section carve-out — and the
helper describes its content, not a code-level special case. The
helper folder name is one of the three explicit forbidden-token
carve-outs in the root spec: it names a non-branching content
anchor, not a dispatch axis.

**Code identifiers and inline prose stay storage-agnostic.** No
identifier in `index.ts` — function name, variable name, parameter
name, file path constant, JSDoc word, log string — uses any of the
forbidden tokens (`vault`, `obsidian`, `note`-as-synonym, `wikilink`,
`hub note`, `hub`). The three structural concepts the module handles
are "playbook entry-point artifact", "study-playbook entry-point
artifact", and "research entry-point artifact". The new
study-playbook step's builder is named `buildStudyPlaybookStep`
(storage-agnostic, mirrors the existing `buildPlaybookStep` and
`buildResearchStep`); the new artifact path constant lives inside
the bundled `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` and resolves to
`coding-playbook/study-playbook.md` for an editor-backed Markdown
brain — that is a template-content fact (the bundled default ships
that path), not a code-level dispatch axis. How the artifacts
manifest in the user's chosen storage is brain.aide's concern, not
this module's. The bundled default brain.aide stays
Obsidian-flavored because that is the most common storage choice,
but the mental model the code expresses is the framework's
storage-agnostic structural shape, not any one connector's specific
shape.

**Storage-agnostic vocabulary across this spec, the module's code,
and every byte it writes into an InitStep.** The module's prose,
outcomes, comments, JSDoc, log strings, error messages, and InitStep
`name` fields all describe the brain in storage-agnostic terms; the
three structural concepts referenced in the module's surface are the
playbook entry-point artifact, the study-playbook entry-point
artifact, and the research entry-point artifact. The two allowed
exceptions are scoped exactly: the `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`
helper folder name (a content anchor for the bundled template, not
a code-level branch target) and the legacy `obsidian` MCP key
referenced inside migration branches (the key being migrated away
from). The `.obsidian` directory heuristic comment in
`brainRootExists` is a third allowed exception inherited from the
root spec but lives in a sibling helper, not in this module's code.
Anti-example occurrences inside undesired-outcome blocks of this
spec are also allowed — naming a forbidden token to forbid it is
the mechanism, not a violation. The parent intent's storage-agnostic
vocabulary outcome carves out the user-owned `studyPlaybook` body
section bytes specifically: the bundled default legitimately ships
the bundled template's connector-specific navigation vocabulary
inside those bytes (the same forbidden tokens the rest of this
spec must avoid appear there by design) because the bundled
template ships that connector and the bytes are user-owned the
moment they land. That carve-out applies to the BYTES (which
become an on-disk artifact the user can edit and override for any
other brain), not to provisionBrain's code surface. Every other
appearance of the forbidden tokens — in this module's code or in
prose surfaces this module owns — is a regression. The default scaffolded brain.aide
ships an Obsidian-flavored body because that is the most common
storage choice, but that is a template-content fact (a user-owned
byte the moment it lands), not a code-identifier fact.

**The legacy `obsidianMcpEntry()` export is removed.** That function
returned a hardcoded launcher object directly. Callers that needed it
now go through the template + `parseBrainAide` + `interpolateArgs`
pipeline. Consumers that previously imported `obsidianMcpEntry`
migrate to derive the entry from a scaffolded or existing brain.aide.

**brain.aide scaffolding is seed-semantic, and the rule is
directory-wide.** The brain.aide file is user-owned the moment it
lands on disk — the user may swap the launcher, retarget the inline
path, rewrite the prose, edit the playbook or research section
bodies, or change the label. A presence check at the expected path
is the correct idempotency signal. The module never returns
`would-overwrite` for brain.aide; doing so would replace a user's
customized config with the package's default on every subsequent
init. The same rule applies to every other file under `.aide/config/`
should one ever be added: the directory itself is the ownership
boundary, inherited from the root spec, with no per-file allowlist.

**Two different idempotency semantics coexist in this module.** The
brain config file, the playbook entry-point artifact, the
study-playbook entry-point artifact, and the research entry-point
artifact use **seed-semantic idempotency**: a simple presence check.
If the file exists at its expected path, the step is `exists` — no
byte comparison, no drift detection against the brain.aide section
that originated it. These files are seeds the user evolves the
moment the brain comes alive; once they own the file, the bytes
belong to them, and the package never proposes replacing them. The
brain MCP prescription uses **canonical-derived idempotency**: the
expected entry is computed from the current brain.aide and compared
against what is in `.mcp.json`. Divergence means `would-overwrite`
(the user may have re-run init before syncing, or the brain.aide
was edited), and the migration branches for legacy `obsidian`-only
and both-keys states fire on the same path. The distinguishing
question is who owns the bytes after first write: for user-curated
content, presence is enough; for the derived MCP entry under
`.mcp.json["brain"]`, the current brain.aide is the source of truth.

**Migration semantics for existing installs.** Cold installs land in
the new shape automatically — the bundled template carries all four
marker-bounded body sections from day one. Two pre-pivot shapes
surface as `malformed-body` from parseBrainAide on the first run
that requires the new sections, because the parser's body grammar
is closed and missing required marker pairs are a structured failure
naming the missing markers. The first pre-pivot shape is the
heading-based body from before the marker-pair refactor (no marker
pairs at all); the second is the three-section marker-bounded body
from the previous cycle (prose + playbook + research, no
study-playbook pair). In the second case `malformed-body` names the
missing `aide-study-playbook-start` / `aide-study-playbook-end`
markers specifically, so the user knows exactly which marker pair
to add. The remediation in either case is for the user to hand-edit
their brain.aide — adding the marker pairs from scratch for the
heading-based body, or adding only the missing study-playbook pair
(and pasting the section content from the new bundled template
default) for the three-section body. provisionBrain does not
silently patch the file under `.aide/config/` to add them, because
that would violate the directory-wide ownership rule. There is no
aide_upgrade carve-out that auto-injects the missing section; the
strict-failure path is intentional.

**Signature takes `projectRoot`, not just `brainPath` and
`mcpConfigPath`.** The pipeline needs the host project root to derive
the brain.aide path at `.aide/config/brain.aide`. Separating
`brainPath` (the brain root) from the project root (where brain.aide
lives) makes the module's contract explicit. The install orchestrator
passes all three values and the single caller update is mechanical.

## Good examples

A cold install on a fresh project. provisionBrain returns six
InitStep items in order:

**Step 1 — Brain config.** Status: would-create. Category: brain.
Name: "Brain config (brain.aide)". FilePath:
`.aide/config/brain.aide` inside the host project root. Content: the
full canonical brain.aide bytes from the bundled template helper,
which on POSIX looks like:

```
---
name: obsidian
mcpServerConfig:
  command: npx
  args:
    - "@bitbonsai/mcpvault"
    - "/home/user/brain"
---

<!-- aide-prose-start -->
Your brain is an external knowledge store reached through MCP tools.
Use `mcp__brain__read_note` to open files by their brain-root-relative
path. Use `mcp__brain__search_notes` for keyword queries across the
brain. The brain's playbook entry-point artifact is at
`coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`; the study-playbook entry-point
artifact (procedural navigation methodology for the playbook) is at
`coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`; the research entry-point
artifact is at `research/research.md`. Read either entry-point
first, then follow the references it lists into the rest of the
brain. ...
<!-- aide-prose-end -->

<!-- aide-playbook-start -->
# Coding Playbook

## Task Routing

| Task domain | Section |
|-------------|---------|

## Always Read First

(content the user fills in)

## Sections

## Contents
<!-- aide-playbook-end -->

<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->
# Study Playbook

## How to use the playbook entry-point

**Step 1.** Open the playbook entry-point artifact. The Task Routing
table tells you which section applies to your task.

**Step 2.** Drill into the section that matches. Read the section
header and the references it lists; pick the references whose names
or descriptions match the task at hand.

**Step 3.** Follow each picked reference into its target. Read only
what your task demands.

## Navigation Rules

The playbook entry-point and section indexes are navigation, not
content — depth does not start there. Depth starts on the first
content reference you reach. From any content reference, follow the
references it lists for at least one or two more levels in any
direction the information could apply, but stop at the first
reference whose target is unrelated to the current task.

(For any backend, the navigation rule "depth starts on the first
content reference" applies regardless of how the references are
encoded — whether they are inline links in an editor-backed brain,
page links in a hosted knowledge base, or path strings in a plain
folder.)
<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->

<!-- aide-research-start -->
# Research

## Domains

| Domain | Files |
|--------|-------|

## How to Use This Index

Research material is filed by domain. Drill into the domain that
matches your task; do not flat-search across every domain. ...
<!-- aide-research-end -->
```

(On Windows the `mcpServerConfig` block becomes `command: cmd` with
`args: ["/c", "npx", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "D:/notes/my-brain"]`.)
The frontmatter has exactly two top-level fields: `name` (a label
only; the package never branches on it) and `mcpServerConfig`. The
brain root path is inlined byte-for-byte in `args` — no `${rootPath}`
placeholder, no separate `rootPath` field, no `entryFile`, no `tools`
block. The body has exactly four marker-bounded sections in the
required prose-then-playbook-then-studyPlaybook-then-research order,
each opened and closed by its own paired HTML-comment markers. The
playbook section above is intentionally trimmed to pure coding
conventions (Task Routing, Always Read First, Sections, Contents)
— the procedural navigation prose lives in the studyPlaybook
section, not interleaved with the playbook section's content rules.
The studyPlaybook section above shows a backend-neutral version of
the navigation methodology; the bundled obsidian-flavored default
ships the same methodology in the bundled template's
connector-specific vocabulary, under the parent intent's user-owned
section-bytes carve-out for the studyPlaybook section. Bytes
outside any marker pair (a blank line between frontmatter and the
first opener, spacing between sections) are silently ignored by
the parser. Any marker layout
violation — missing pair, typo'd token, unmatched closer, wrong
section order, nested markers — would surface as `malformed-body`
from the parser naming the offending marker.

**Step 2 — Brain root directories.** Status: would-create. Category:
brain. FilePath: the brain root. Content: a JSON array of the
directory paths to create.

**Step 3 — Playbook entry-point artifact.** Status: would-create.
Category: brain. FilePath: the playbook entry-point artifact's path
inside the brain root (e.g., `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md` for
an editor-backed Markdown brain — the storage shape is dictated by
the user's chosen backend, not by this module). Content: sourced from
`parseBrainAideFromString(brainAideStep.content).playbook` — the
verbatim bytes between `<!-- aide-playbook-start -->` and
`<!-- aide-playbook-end -->` in the brain.aide that Step 1 just
planned. The bytes are NOT a copy of an inline TypeScript constant
in this module; they came from the brain.aide playbook section, full
stop.

**Step 4 — Study-playbook entry-point artifact.** Status:
would-create. Category: brain. FilePath: the study-playbook
entry-point artifact's path inside the brain root (e.g.,
`coding-playbook/study-playbook.md` for an editor-backed Markdown
brain — the same `coding-playbook/` directory the playbook
entry-point artifact lives in; the storage shape is dictated by the
user's chosen backend, not by this module). Content: sourced from
`parseBrainAideFromString(brainAideStep.content).studyPlaybook` —
the verbatim bytes between `<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` and
`<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->` in the brain.aide that Step 1
just planned. Same parser pass as Step 3, different field, different
artifact path. The bytes are NOT a copy of an inline TypeScript
constant in this module; they came from the brain.aide
studyPlaybook section, full stop. The artifact at this path is the
on-disk surface the agent-side `study-playbook` skill points at —
the skill stays minimal because the navigation methodology lives
in this artifact.

**Step 5 — Research entry-point artifact.** Status: would-create.
Category: brain. FilePath: the research entry-point artifact's path
inside the brain root (e.g., `research/research.md` for an
editor-backed Markdown brain). Content: sourced from
`parseBrainAideFromString(brainAideStep.content).research` — the
verbatim bytes between `<!-- aide-research-start -->` and
`<!-- aide-research-end -->`. Same parser pass as Steps 3 and 4,
different field, different artifact path.

**Step 6 — MCP config (brain).** Status: would-create. Category:
mcp. Prescription derived from the same Step 1 content bytes:
parseBrainAide parses the template string, interpolateArgs walks
`mcpServerConfig.args` (which contains no `${...}` placeholders on
the default scaffold, so the args pass through unchanged), and the
resulting entry on POSIX is:

```
{
  command: "npx",
  args: ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "/home/user/brain"]
}
```

(On Windows: `{ command: "cmd", args: ["/c", "npx",
"@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "D:/notes/my-brain"] }`.) The args list is
byte-identical to what the template wrote to disk — interpolateArgs
runs but has nothing to substitute on the default scaffold.

Re-running on the same project after the first install: all six
steps return `exists`. The brain.aide presence check fires (the file
is on disk under `.aide/config/`); the brain root directories are
present; the playbook, study-playbook, and research entry-point
artifacts are present (presence-only check on each, no byte
comparison against the current brain.aide section that originated
them); the MCP config `brain` key matches the derived entry.

Running on a brain where the user has hand-edited their brain.aide
to retarget the brain root by changing the inline path string in
`mcpServerConfig.args`: the brain config step is `exists` (the file
is present under `.aide/config/`); the brain root directory step
reports based on the new path; the MCP step derives its expected
entry from the user's edited brain.aide, NOT from the bundled
template default — the user's customization flows through
automatically. The entry-point artifacts at the new brain root
either exist (if the user already moved them) and report `exists`,
or are absent and report `would-create` with content derived from
the user's current `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, and `research` body
sections respectively. The study-playbook artifact at
`coding-playbook/study-playbook.md` follows the same per-artifact
rule: present → `exists` regardless of any drift between the disk
file and the user's current studyPlaybook section bytes; absent →
`would-create` with content sourced from the studyPlaybook section
of the edited brain.aide. An advanced user who instead pulls the
path into a (now custom) top-level frontmatter field and references
it in `args` via `${myPath}` exercises the `${...}` interpolation
surface; `interpolateArgs` substitutes the value and the derived
entry's args carry the resolved string.

A migration run on a legacy install (entry previously written under
`obsidian` key, no `brain` key): the MCP step reports
`would-overwrite` with `prescription: { key: "brain", entry: <derived
from brain.aide> }`. The other five steps run their normal
idempotency checks. After apply, the legacy `obsidian` key is
replaced by the canonical `brain` key. The both-keys-present
transitional state takes the same `would-overwrite` branch with the
same prescription.

A migration run on a pre-pivot three-section install (brain.aide
present under `.aide/config/`, valid frontmatter, body carries the
prose, playbook, and research marker pairs but no study-playbook
pair): parseBrainAide returns `malformed-body` naming the missing
`aide-study-playbook-start` / `aide-study-playbook-end` markers
specifically. provisionBrain does not silently patch the file under
`.aide/config/` to inject the missing pair, because that would
violate the directory-wide ownership rule. Remediation is a
hand-edit by the user — typically pasting the studyPlaybook section
content from the new bundled template default between the playbook
and research sections — after which the next run plans all six
steps normally.

## Bad examples

A provisionBrain that hardcodes entry-point artifact bodies as inline
TypeScript constants:

```
// Wrong — entry-point artifact bytes outside the brain.aide body
const PLAYBOOK_TEMPLATE = `# Coding Playbook\n\n## Task Routing...`;
const STUDY_PLAYBOOK_TEMPLATE = `# Study Playbook\n\n## Step 1...`;
const RESEARCH_TEMPLATE = `# Research\n\n## Domains...`;

steps.push({ name: "Playbook entry-point", content: PLAYBOOK_TEMPLATE });
steps.push({ name: "Study-playbook entry-point", content: STUDY_PLAYBOOK_TEMPLATE });
steps.push({ name: "Research entry-point", content: RESEARCH_TEMPLATE });
```

The package now owns three top-level inline TypeScript constants in
`index.ts` (one per entry-point artifact). A user who edits their
brain.aide's `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, or `research` body section
to add their own routing rows, navigation tweaks, or domain entries
finds the next install ignores their edit and lands the package
default again. This is the same regression as the legacy
launcher-bytes duplication the schema collapse already retired,
applied to a different kind of byte. Shipping a
`STUDY_PLAYBOOK_TEMPLATE` constant alongside the playbook and
research siblings is the same regression in different clothing —
the third artifact is the new entry in the contract, not a new
exception to it. Entry-point artifact bytes belong in the named
marker-bounded brain.aide body sections and nowhere else in this
module.

A provisionBrain that scaffolds a brain-root CLAUDE.md as part of the
brain contract:

```
// Wrong — CLAUDE.md is no longer a framework requirement
steps.push({
  name: "Brain-root CLAUDE.md",
  filePath: join(brainPath, "CLAUDE.md"),
  content: BRAIN_CLAUDE_MD_TEMPLATE, // navigation entry-point bytes
});
```

The framework's brain contract is exactly three required
entry-point artifacts (playbook + study-playbook + research). A
brain-root CLAUDE.md is a user choice, not a framework requirement,
and the navigation methodology that used to live in that file
(link-crawling protocols, decision protocols, where-to-find-things
tables) now flows through the `studyPlaybook` body section and the
on-disk artifact it scaffolds. Reintroducing a CLAUDE.md scaffolding
step under any name — "brain-root CLAUDE.md", "navigation
entry-point", "navigation hub" — restores the retired contract.
Users who want a CLAUDE.md in their brain add one themselves; the
package does not plan one. (The token "vault" appears nowhere here
because the retired naming has been swept; the prohibition still
holds against any rename that would reintroduce a CLAUDE.md
scaffolding step.)

A provisionBrain that uses storage-specific identifiers in code:

```
// Wrong — connector-specific tokens in code identifiers
async function vaultExists(brainPath: string): Promise<boolean> { ... }
const VAULT_DIRS = ["research", "process/retro", "coding-playbook"];
const obsidianHubPath = join(brainPath, "coding-playbook", "coding-playbook.md");
const obsidianStudyPlaybookHub = join(brainPath, "coding-playbook", "study-playbook.md");
```

The mental model these names express is one connector's, not the
framework's. A user whose brain is a hosted knowledge base, a plain
folder, or any storage other than the default still needs the
playbook, study-playbook, and research entry-point artifacts to
land somewhere; the code that plans those landings should not assume
the landing is a `.md` file inside an editor-backed brain. The
structural concepts the module handles are "brain root", "playbook
entry-point artifact", "study-playbook entry-point artifact", and
"research entry-point artifact" — how those manifest in the user's
chosen storage is brain.aide's concern, expressed in the body
sections the user owns. The bundled default template can be
Obsidian-flavored because that is the most common storage choice,
but no identifier or string constant in `index.ts` may name any of
the forbidden tokens. Helpers that retain the retired
storage-specific tokens in their names — and any helper named with
those tokens whether as `*Exists`, a `*_DIRS` constant, an
`obsidian*HubPath` derivation, or an `obsidianStudyPlaybookHub`
landing-path constant — are the same regression in different
clothing; the `Hub` suffix regression class extends to the new
study-playbook artifact's path identifier just as it applies to the
playbook artifact's. Each must be renamed to the storage-agnostic
shape the module's code already uses elsewhere.

A provisionBrain that embeds link-crawling navigation prose as an
inline constant alongside the brain.aide-sourced studyPlaybook step:

```
// Wrong — navigation methodology duplicated as a sibling install constant
const NAVIGATION_PROSE = `When reading any entry-point file, follow
the inline references it lists that are relevant to your task.
Entry-point files are navigation; content files are where depth
starts...`;

steps.push({
  name: "Study-playbook entry-point",
  filePath: studyPlaybookArtifactPath,
  content: NAVIGATION_PROSE, // ← bytes that should come from brain.aide
});
```

The navigation methodology IS the studyPlaybook section bytes —
those bytes ship through the bundled brain.aide template helper's
`studyPlaybook` body section and reach the install module exactly
once, by reading them back through parseBrainAide and writing them
out as the study-playbook entry-point artifact's `content`.
Duplicating the same prose as a sibling inline `NAVIGATION_PROSE`
constant in `index.ts` creates two sources of truth for the same
bytes — the moment the bundled template's studyPlaybook section
evolves (a new step added to the navigation methodology, the
depth-counting example refined, the link-traversal rules tightened)
the inline copy silently diverges. A user who edits their
brain.aide's `studyPlaybook` body section to tune the navigation
methodology for their own brain finds the next install ignores
their edit and lands the package's compiled NAVIGATION_PROSE
constant on the disk artifact instead. The same regression class
applies whether the inline constant is named `NAVIGATION_PROSE`,
`STUDY_PLAYBOOK_PROSE`, `LINK_CRAWLING_RULES`, or any other
rename: navigation methodology lives in the bundled template's
studyPlaybook section bytes only; the install module reads it back
through parseBrainAide and never authors a parallel copy.

A provisionBrain that skips the brain.aide step and constructs the
MCP entry inline:

```
// Wrong — reconstructs launcher directly
const entry = obsidianMcpEntry(brainPath);
steps.push({ name: "MCP config (brain)", prescription: { key: "brain", entry } });
```

The user's brain.aide (which may have a custom launcher, a different
label, or a different inline path) is ignored. The package's compiled
constant wins every time, defeating the user-ownership model the
refactor was built around.

A provisionBrain that hardcodes the Windows launcher shape in
index.ts:

```
// Wrong — launcher bytes outside the template helper
const command = process.platform === "win32" ? "cmd" : "npx";
const args = process.platform === "win32"
  ? ["/c", "npx", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", brainPath]
  : ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", brainPath];
```

The canonical launcher bytes now live in two places. When the
template helper changes (say, a new flag is added to the args list,
or the default launcher is swapped out for something else), this
inline copy silently diverges and the scaffolded brain.aide no
longer matches what the MCP step would compute for a cold install.
The same rejection applies to any inline copy of the legacy
`obsidian-mcp` launcher — duplicating launcher bytes outside the
template is the bug, not the specific launcher being duplicated.

A provisionBrain that returns `would-overwrite` for the seed
brain.aide:

```
// Wrong — byte-compares a user-owned config file
const disk = await readFile(brainAidePath, "utf8");
const template = bundledBrainAideTemplate(brainPath);
if (disk !== template) {
  return { name: "Brain config (brain.aide)", status: "would-overwrite", content: template };
}
```

The user edited their brain.aide to point at a new brain root. On
the next init, they are asked "overwrite your brain config?" A
reflexive Enter replaces their custom inline path with the stale
default from the template. This is data loss from a pattern that
only looks safe — and the same failure mode applies to any future
file scaffolded under `.aide/config/`: the directory boundary is the
ownership signal, and no code path in this module is allowed to
return `would-overwrite` for any path inside it.

A provisionBrain that scaffolds a default brain.aide using
`${rootPath}` substitution:

```
// Wrong — default scaffold exercises the advanced-user interpolation surface
return bundledBrainAideTemplate(brainPath); // emits args: ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "${rootPath}"]
```

The default scaffold's job is to produce a working brain config that
the user can read top-to-bottom without learning a templating DSL.
Substitution is the opt-in path for advanced users who want to DRY a
value across positions; baking it into the default forces every user
into the advanced workflow before they have ever touched their
config.

A brain.aide scaffolded with intent-spec frontmatter fields:

```
---
scope: .aide/config/brain.aide # Wrong — no scope in brain.aide
intent: configures the brain   # Wrong — no intent paragraph
outcomes:                      # Wrong — no outcomes block
  desired:
    - brain is wired
name: obsidian
...
```

Every downstream agent that reads this file now has to decide which
rules apply — the brain.aide config rules or the intent-spec rules.
The distinction between config files and intent specs is load-bearing
and must be enforced at the scaffolding layer.

A brain.aide scaffolded with extra frontmatter fields beyond the
collapsed schema:

```
---
name: obsidian
connector: obsidian            # Wrong — field renamed away; `name` is the only label
rootPath: D:/notes/my-brain    # Wrong — path lives inline in args, not as its own field
entryFile: CLAUDE.md           # Wrong — no code consumes this
mcpServerConfig:
  command: npx
  args: ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "${rootPath}"]
tools:                         # Wrong — interface contract lives in the prose body
  read: mcp__brain__read_note
  search: mcp__brain__search_notes
---
```

Each removed field is validation theater — a slot the user is nagged
to fill in with no code path that consumes the value once filled.
The schema collapse is load-bearing: every frontmatter field must
justify itself by naming a real runtime consumer (sync writes
`mcpServerConfig`; the user and agent read `name` as a label). The
agent-facing interface (which MCP tool names to call, where to start
reading, how to follow links) lives in the prose body where the
agent actually reads it.

## References

- `.aide/intent.aide` (frontmatter and broader brain-layer outcomes)
  — Parent spec: brain.aide as the single source of truth for brain
  configuration, the collapsed two-field schema (`name` +
  `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}`), the `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`
  default launcher, the `.aide/config/` directory ownership boundary,
  the sync contract as the only mutation surface for `.mcp.json`,
  the no-substitution-in-the-default-scaffold rule, and the
  outcomes.undesired rule that brain.aide must not borrow intent-spec
  frontmatter conventions or add validation-theater fields. Informed
  the directory-wide ownership invariant, the schema-minimum-equals-
  maximum rule, and the no-connector-branching constraint that drives
  storage-agnostic identifier naming in this module. The body-grammar
  outcome (intent #4) and the entry-point-artifact-contract outcome
  (intent #7) are both undergoing the queued amendment from three
  sections / two artifacts to four sections / three artifacts as
  documented in `.aide/handoff.aide`'s "Major code refactor queued"
  subsection; this spec's Strategy and Good examples already track
  the post-amendment shape.
- `.aide/intent.aide` (outcome on the body schema) — Parent spec:
  established that the body of `brain.aide` is parsed as named
  marker-bounded sections delimited by paired HTML-comment markers
  recognized lowercase and case-sensitive in fixed order, with
  marker layout violations halting the pipeline as `malformed-body`
  naming the offending marker and bytes outside any marker pair
  silently ignored. The queued amendment in `.aide/handoff.aide`
  raises the section count from three to four and inserts the
  `<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` / `<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->`
  pair between the playbook and research pairs (intent #4); the
  required order becomes prose-then-playbook-then-studyPlaybook-
  then-research. Drove the marker-bounded body vocabulary across
  this spec's Strategy, the four-section worked Good example, and
  the Bad-example regression scenarios.
- `.aide/intent.aide` (outcome on storage-agnostic vocabulary) —
  Parent spec: established the forbidden-token list (`vault`,
  `obsidian`, `note`-as-synonym, `wikilink`, `hub note`, `hub`) and
  the rule that those tokens must not appear in any user-facing
  prose surface or in code identifiers, comments, or string
  constants project-wide. Field-name expansion under the queued
  amendment widens the storage-agnostic field set to `playbook`,
  `studyPlaybook` (camelCase, no `Hub` suffix), and `research`. The
  parent intent's user-owned-section-bytes carve-out specifically
  permits the bundled obsidian-flavored default to ship
  obsidian-flavored navigation prose inside the studyPlaybook
  section bytes (the bytes are user-owned the moment they land);
  the carve-out applies to the bytes, not to provisionBrain's code
  surface. Drove the Strategy beat on storage-agnostic vocabulary
  in this spec, the field-name shift away from retired suffixed
  forms, the Bad-example rewrite of identifier-naming and
  navigation-prose-duplication scenarios (including the
  `obsidianStudyPlaybookHub` regression class extension), and the
  anti-example carve-out scoping (anti-example narratives that
  explicitly forbid a token are allowed; generic prose stays clean).
- `src/service/parseBrainAide/.aide` — Sibling spec: the parser that
  reads brain.aide and returns typed frontmatter plus four named
  body fields (`prose`, `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, `research`)
  extracted from paired HTML-comment markers, plus the
  interpolateArgs helper that walks `mcpServerConfig.args`
  substituting `${...}` references against frontmatter values. The
  parser spec's per-consumer-ownership outcomes name provisionBrain
  as the consumer that reads `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, AND
  `research` to source the three required entry-point artifact
  bodies — every artifact's `content` field traces back through
  this parser to a brain.aide body section, never to an inline
  TypeScript constant. Informed the strict-failure migration
  semantics for existing installs whose brain.aide carries either
  the pre-pivot heading-based body (no markers at all) or the
  pre-amendment three-section marker-bounded body (no
  study-playbook pair) — parseBrainAide returns `malformed-body`
  naming the specific missing markers in either case.
- `src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/` —
  Sibling helper: the bundled default brain.aide template. Now owns
  the collapsed two-field frontmatter, the platform-branched
  launcher invocation, AND the byte content of all four
  marker-bounded body sections (`prose`, `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`,
  `research`). The `playbook` section bytes are trimmed to pure
  coding conventions (Task Routing, Always Read First, Sections,
  Contents); the `studyPlaybook` section bytes hold the
  multi-month-tested backend-specific playbook-navigation prose
  (the Step 1/2/3 process, the Navigation Rules block with the
  depth-counting example, link-traversal semantics — content the
  package owns by design because it travels with the bundled
  obsidian-flavored default). Informed the strategy decision that
  this helper's responsibilities expand to include the trimmed
  playbook section, the new studyPlaybook section, and the
  unchanged research section, and that the helper folder name
  remains one of the explicit forbidden-token carve-outs (a
  non-branching content anchor for the bundled template, not a
  dispatch axis).
- `.aide/handoff.aide` — Post-collapse architectural snapshot: the
  "Major code refactor queued" subsection on 2026-04-26 documents
